{
	title: '/logout revokes web logins',
	spec: "/clients names each web login (device, browser, first and last seen); /logout <client> or /logout all revokes them by deleting their token hashes from state/web-sessions.ason, and a revoked browser's open sockets close at once and fall back to the login page. The current browser can log itself out from the web status details. Replaces the TODO.md item.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Restored invariant: a revoked login cannot authenticate HTTP or WebSocket requests, receive further session events, or submit commands through existing sockets. Hashes are deleted before socket teardown; upgrades recheck after async page builds and at socket open, and messages recheck authentication. Tests cover targeted and all-login revocation, two sockets sharing a login, restart persistence, failed reconnection, the build/revocation race, and same-origin POST self-logout. Existing context event streams all close on revocation as a conservative security measure.',
		'Login records retain only token hashes and carry independent public 10-character ids, device/browser labels and first/last seen. Expiry-only legacy records migrate without token rotation; unknown dates/device are stated, not invented. The status-details action is a native POST form, reusing existing 44px buttons; real Chrome checks phone/desktop fit and return to the login gate. /logout is shared by terminal and web; the browser-only self action is explicit in the spec. The new command makes /lo and /log ambiguous, so the completion retention test uses /logi then /login instead.'
	],
	needs: []
}
